      "budget_share": 0.6,
      "why": "unrolled_k is the family that can actually satisfy the system constraints. A 32-request burst arriving every 1us must be fully drained within 0.5us (p99_batch), which requires accepting multiple requests per cycle: with k micro-rotations/cycle the unit processes k results per cycle, so the burst drains in ceil(32/k) cycles and the per-cycle throughput is k*Fmax. Larger k raises throughput and shortens the burst window (lower p99_batch, lower stall) at the cost of a wider combinational datapath (lower Fmax, higher area). Sweeping k=2..8 across the accuracy-feasible data_width/n_iter band is where the real area-vs-system-constraint trade-off lives, and it is what the hypervolume needs to cover. I weight it higher than iterative because it is the only family that can meet the batch/throughput gates, so the feasible front is dominated by it."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "I am restricted to iterative and unrolled_k. The spec has three hard gates: (1) throughput_msps >= 32, (2) max_abs_err <= 2^-12 (accuracy_bits >= 12), and (3) sys_p99_batch_us <= 0.5 for a 32-request burst every 1us. Accuracy depends only on data_width/n_iter/angle_guard/frac_guard/rounding, so I bound those to the band that can reach 12 bits while keeping area low (data_width 14-20, n_iter 14-20, small guards). The distinguishing variable is throughput/burst handling: iterative (1 result/cycle) is the area floor but can only meet the gates if Fmax is high enough that 32 results fit in 0.5us and average 32 Msps; unrolled_k (k results/cycle) is the family that robustly meets the burst and throughput gates, with k as the main area-vs-throughput knob. I therefore map iterative for the area-minimising corner (selection rule = min area) and spend the larger share on unrolled_k to cover the k-sweep trade-off that the hypervolume rewards. No design is assumed feasible yet; the search will confirm which (family, k, width) points actually pass all three constraints, and if none do I will report infeasible rather than relax them."
}

